Core Linux Administration

1. Difference between ext3 and ext4
ext3: Older, no support for extents, slower fsck.
ext4: Faster, supports extents, larger file size, journaling improvements.

2. Runlevels in Linux
Runlevel 3: Multi-user, non-GUI, full networking.
Runlevel 5: Multi-user with GUI.
Can check using: runlevel or systemctl get-default (on systemd).

3. What is fstab?
A file located at /etc/fstab used to define how disk partitions, remote filesystems are mounted at boot.

4. Check disk usage
df -h        # Disk usage by filesystem
du -sh /path # Size of a specific directory

5. What is an inode?
Metadata about a file: owner, permissions, timestamps (but NOT filename).
Check inode usage: df -i

6. Find large files
find / -type f -size +500M -exec ls -lh {} \; | sort -k 5 -rh | head

 LVM & File Systems

7. Extend a logical volume
extend -L +5G /dev/vgname/lvname
resize2fs /dev/vgname/lvname    # for ext4
xfs_growfs /mount/point         # for XFS

8. Shrink a logical volume (ext4 only)
umount /mount/point
e2fsck -f /dev/vgname/lvname
resize2fs /dev/vgname/lvname 5G
lvreduce -L 5G /dev/vgname/lvname
mount /mount/point

9. xfs_growfs vs resize2fs

xfs_growfs: Used for XFS filesystem (only supports grow).

resize2fs: Used for ext2/3/4 (supports shrink and grow).



